<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pungnap Toseong</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pungnaptoseong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pungnap Toseong is a large earthen-walled site on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> in Songpa, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, built around the third century CE. Its walls, about 3.5 kilometres around, enclosed a royal centre, and many historians identify it as the northern fortress of Hanseong, the capital of Baekje until its fall to Goguryeo in 475. Excavations have found palace and ritual buildings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pungnap-toseong.jpg" alt="Pungnap Toseong" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Straitgate · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pungnap_Toseong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Archaeologically, the earthen walls of <b>Pungnap Toseong</b> and Mongchon Toseong in south-eastern Seoul, raised around the 3rd century CE, mark the emergence of a sizeable royal centre, and most historians date Baekje's development into a true kingdom to the reign of King Goi (traditionally 234–286) or later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/baekje">Baekje · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
